.TH CBMAP_INIT_CC 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cbmap_init_cc \- create an ordered map with a custom comparison procedure into an already declared handle
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cbstmap.h>

cbmap_init_cc(hm_name, custom_comparison_proc);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Creates an ordered map. custom_comparison_proc orders the keys of that map,
and not the default order for the declared key type. The macro then assigns
the map to hm_name. The caller must declare the handle first, with
cbmap_declare(3) or cbmap_declare_scoped(3). The key type and the value type
do not come to this macro as arguments. The macro reads them from the
companion type variables that the declare macro created.

The macro is cbmap_init(3) plus a comparison procedure. It uses the default
allocator. cbmap_init_mp(3) replaces the allocator instead, and
cbmap_init_full(3) takes both.

A struct key type needs this variant. Without a procedure, the map orders a
key type that it does not recognize by a raw comparison of the
representation. Such a comparison reads padding bytes. It also orders a
pointer member by its address.

The one-step equivalents are cbmap_construct_cc(3) and
cbmap_construct_cc_scoped(3).
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name                 The map handle that the caller declared before.
                        The macro creates the map into it
custom_comparison_proc  A ccol_comparison_proc_t, or NULL for the
                        default order for the declared key type
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. This macro expands to a statement and does not expand to an
expression.
.fi
.SH "CUSTOM COMPARISON"
.nf
The procedure gets pointers to two keys. It returns a negative value when
the first key orders before the second key. It returns a positive value when
the first key orders after the second key. It returns zero when the map must
treat the two keys as the same key. A zero makes a call an update and not an
insert. The procedure therefore defines key equality and key order.

The procedure must give a strict weak order over the key type. That order
must not change while the map holds elements. The same pair of keys must
always compare in the same way. A comparison in the opposite direction must
give the opposite sign. The order must also be transitive. An inconsistent
procedure breaks the placement invariant of the tree. After that, the map
cannot find a key that it holds.

The procedure gets two bare pointers and no lengths. It can only read a
width that the declared key type fixes. The map therefore validates the
width of an incoming key against that type. The map also aligns its stored
keys for that type. The procedure decides the order of the keys and nothing
else.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro stops the program through ccol_fatal_err() if the creation fails
The macro uses the default allocator
The procedure must stay callable for the whole life of the map
The order of the walk follows this procedure. The walk visits the elements
in ascending key order, by the order that the map got.
cbmap_begin_iter(3) describes that contract
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
typedef struct { int major; int minor; } version;

int version_cmp(const void *a, const void *b) {
    version x, y;
    memcpy(&x, a, sizeof(x));   /* a key can be at any address */
    memcpy(&y, b, sizeof(y));
    if (x.major != y.major) {
        return (x.major > y.major) - (x.major < y.major);
    }
    return (x.minor > y.minor) - (x.minor < y.minor);
}

cbmap_declare(releases, version, char *);
cbmap_init_cc(releases, version_cmp);
cbmap_destroy(releases);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
